Key aspects of the policy include:

Applicability:
Covers policy banks, commercial banks, rural cooperative banks, and credit unions.
Encourages non-bank financial institutions to adopt similar principles.
Core Requirements:
Strategic Integration: Banks must align their lending policies with environmental protection goals and national green development plans.
Environmental & Social Risk Management: Institutions must establish risk assessment systems to evaluate the climate, pollution, and social impact of borrowers.
Green Finance Promotion: Priority should be given to green industries, including renewable energy, circular economy projects, and energy efficiency upgrades.
Exclusion Policies:
Banks must not lend to projects that violate environmental regulations or pose high ecological risks.
Transparency & Disclosure:
Banks must publicly disclose their green credit strategies.
Annual self-assessment reports on green credit must be submitted to regulators.
Oversight & Enforcement:
The CBIRC conducts periodic audits and integrates green credit performance into bank ratings and regulatory approvals.
Violations may result in restrictions on market access, penalties, or supervisory actions.', 'The Green Credit Guidelines, issued by the China Banking and Insurance Regulatory Commission (CBIRC) in 2012, establish a framework for banks and financial institutions to incorporate environmental and social risk management into their lending activities. The guidelines aim to promote sustainable finance, support low-carbon economic development, and prevent financial risks associated with environmental and social issues.

